// The SILENT half of the uninstantiated-template probe, and the reason this fixture is worth as much as
// the three `xfail/generic_uninst_*` ones: a pass about rejection is only as good as the valid code it
// leaves alone. Every generic below is never instantiated, so `checkUninstantiatedTemplates` walks all of
// them; every one is correct, so it must say nothing.
//
// Measured while this pass was being built: before unknown-type diagnostics learned to defer, the walk
// rejected 81 of 640 corpus fixtures — all of them valid — on four rules that fire on an UNRESOLVED type
// rather than a wrong one. Each shape below is one of those four.
//
// ⚠️ TWO OF THESE WERE NOT VALID, and this fixture asserted they were for as long as it has existed.
// Once opaque type parameters made `T` a real type, `DynamicArray<T>` became an ordinary instance and the
// ordinary rules reached it: case 1 borrowed a view with nothing bounding its storage, and case 3 called
// a `get` that `DynamicArray` does not have. Both are wrong at EVERY instantiation — the concrete
// spelling of case 1 has been rejected since the view-window campaign shipped. That is the whole point of
// the pass, found in the one fixture built to prove it was not needed.
import std::collections::{DynamicArray, View};

// 1. a method call on a `T`-parameterized receiver — `DynamicArray<T>` resolves through the opaque
//    parameter, so the view-window rule applies here exactly as it does to `DynamicArray<int32>`: a view
//    local must be rooted in a `borrow` window that holds its storage still.
fn isize neverCalled1<T>(ref DynamicArray<T> d) {
    isize n = d.length();
    borrow d.view() as v {
        n = n + v.length();
    }
    return n;
}

// 2. a turbofish forwarding the enclosing type parameter — no instantiation to route to
fn void neverCalled2<T>(ref DynamicArray<T> d) {
    neverCalled1::<T>(d: d);
}

// 3. dot-on-type construction of a generic whose arguments come from the enclosing template.
//    `remove` is the element read that HANDS THE ELEMENT OVER, which is what `give` into `add` needs —
//    and what an unbounded `T` supports, since nothing here promises it is copyable.
fn void neverCalled3<T>(ref DynamicArray<T> src) {
    DynamicArray<T> d = DynamicArray.empty();
    d.add(item: give src.remove(index: 0));
}

// 4. mutual recursion between two templates — the walk must terminate, not chase instantiations
fn void pingNeverCalled<T>(T x) { pongNeverCalled::<T>(x: x); }
fn void pongNeverCalled<T>(T x) { pingNeverCalled::<T>(x: x); }

fn int32 main() { return 0; }
